// Base64-encoded Noto Sans Gujarati (static Regular instance,
// weight 400, width 100) for embedding into jsPDF-generated PDFs.
//
// Why this exists: jsPDF ships only Latin-only standard fonts
// (Helvetica/Times/Courier) -- any Gujarati text (goods types, entry
// number labels, vepari names, etc.) rendered without an embedded
// font shows as blank boxes or missing glyphs in the exported PDF,
// even though the same text displays fine in the app itself and in
// Excel/CSV exports (which just store Unicode text -- no
// font-embedding problem there).
//
// Sourced from Google Fonts' own source repo (ofl/notosansgujarati,
// SIL Open Font License), NOT from the @fontsource npm package --
// @fontsource ships this font pre-split into separate per-script
// subset files (a "gujarati" subset with no Latin/digit glyphs at
// all, a separate "latin" subset, etc. -- a web-loading optimization
// via CSS unicode-range that doesn't help when embedding ONE font
// into a PDF that needs to render both scripts together). The
// upstream source is a variable font; instanced down to a single
// static Regular (wght 400, wdth 100) weight via fonttools, since
// jsPDF's font embedding expects a static TTF, not a variable one.
// See memory.md's decisions log for the full story.

export const NOTO_SANS_GUJARATI_REGULAR_BASE64 =
  ""
